\title{Exact Zero Knowledge and Special Soundness\\for a Three-Move Homomorphism Protocol}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a three-move identification protocol for a public homomorphism $\varphi:G\to H$ between additive commutative groups. The public statement is an element $y\in H$, and a witness is a preimage $w\in G$ satisfying $\varphi(w)=y$. An honest prover commits with $\varphi(r)$ for random $r\in G$, receives a Boolean challenge $c$, and responds with $r+[c]w$. We establish perfect completeness, unconditional acceptance of simulated transcripts, exact perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge over finite $G$, and special soundness. Translation by the challenge-dependent witness term gives an explicit bijection between real and simulated random tapes, proving equality of transcript multisets. Accepted responses to both challenges for one commitment yield the witness $z_1-z_0$. Consequently, when no witness exists, a fixed commitment cannot answer both challenges. We give finite cyclic-group algorithms and examples and distinguish this result from broader claims about zero-knowledge certification of arbitrary mathematical proofs.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A proof ordinarily persuades by disclosure: the verifier inspects the argument. A zero-knowledge protocol separates persuasion from disclosure. It allows a prover to demonstrate possession of a witness while ensuring that the verifier's view contains no information beyond what can be generated without that witness.

The phrase ``contains no information'' requires a distributional definition. It is not enough that the transcript omits the witness or that extraction appears difficult. The simulation paradigm asks for an algorithm that produces the verifier's view without the witness. In the strongest finite setting, simulated and real distributions agree exactly. Perfect zero knowledge then rules out every statistical distinguisher, even one with unbounded computation.

This paper presents an elementary protocol in which completeness, privacy, and extraction reduce to group identities. Let $G,H$ be additive commutative groups and $\varphi:G\to H$ a homomorphism. The public target is $y\in H$; a witness is $w\in G$ with $\varphi(w)=y$. The prover masks $w$ with a random group element. A Boolean challenge requests either the mask or the mask shifted by the witness.

We prove four exact results: every honest execution is accepted; a simulator creates accepted transcripts without a witness; over finite $G$, real and simulated transcript distributions are identical; and two accepted answers to opposite challenges for one commitment reveal a witness. A single view is simulatable, while two compatible views are extractable.

The scope is precise. This is an information-theoretic analysis of a homomorphic preimage identification protocol. It is not by itself a succinct zero-knowledge protocol for arbitrary theorems. That broader goal requires proof encodings, commitments, general zero-knowledge transformations, complexity bounds, and protection against malicious verifiers.

\section{Algebraic setting}
Let $(G,+,0,-)$ and $(H,+,0,-)$ be commutative groups, and let $\varphi:G\to H$ satisfy
\[
\varphi(x+x')=\varphi(x)+\varphi(x').
\]
Thus $\varphi(0)=0$ and $\varphi(x-x')=\varphi(x)-\varphi(x')$.

\begin{definition}[Public statement and witness]
A public statement is a pair $(\varphi,y)$ consisting of a homomorphism $\varphi:G\to H$ and target $y\in H$. An element $w\in G$ is a witness when $\varphi(w)=y$. The associated language is $\operatorname{im}(\varphi)$.
\end{definition}

For $c\in\bool$ and any element $x$ of an additive group, define
\[
[c]x=\begin{cases}0,&c=0,\\x,&c=1.\end{cases}
\]
Case analysis gives $\varphi([c]x)=[c]\varphi(x)$.

\begin{definition}[Transcript and acceptance]
A transcript is $(a,c,z)\in H\times\bool\times G$, where $a$ is a commitment, $c$ a challenge, and $z$ a response. It is accepted precisely when
\[
\varphi(z)=a+[c]y.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{Protocol and completeness}
Given $(\varphi,y)$ and witness $w$, the protocol is:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item sample $r\in G$ uniformly and send $a=\varphi(r)$;
\item receive a uniformly sampled challenge $c\in\bool$;
\item send $z=r+[c]w$;
\item accept exactly when $\varphi(z)=a+[c]y$.
\end{enumerate}
For fixed $w,r,c$, write
\[
\Rtrans(w,r,c)=\bigl(\varphi(r),c,r+[c]w\bigr).
\]
The commitment must precede the challenge. Otherwise accepted transcripts could be constructed backward without evidence of prior knowledge.

\begin{theorem}[Perfect completeness]
If $\varphi(w)=y$, then for every $r\in G$ and $c\in\bool$, the transcript $\Rtrans(w,r,c)$ is accepted.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $z=r+[c]w$,
\[
\varphi(z)=\varphi(r)+\varphi([c]w)
=\varphi(r)+[c]\varphi(w)
=\varphi(r)+[c]y.
\]
The commitment is $a=\varphi(r)$, so this is the acceptance equation. The result holds for every random tape and challenge, hence completeness is perfect.
\end{proof}

\section{Simulation and exact zero knowledge}
The simulator fixes $c$, chooses the response first, and derives a compatible commitment.

\begin{definition}[Simulated transcript]
For $z\in G$ and $c\in\bool$, define
\[
\Strans(z,c)=\bigl(\varphi(z)-[c]y,c,z\bigr).
\]
This uses public information only.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universal simulator acceptance]
Every simulated transcript is accepted, whether or not a witness exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Its commitment is $a=\varphi(z)-[c]y$, and therefore
\[
a+[c]y=\varphi(z)-[c]y+[c]y=\varphi(z).
\]
\end{proof}

This does not violate soundness: simulation fixes the challenge before choosing the commitment, whereas a real prover commits first.

Fix a valid witness $w$ and challenge $c$. Define
\[
\tau_{c,w}(r)=r+[c]w.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Translation bijection]
The map $\tau_{c,w}:G\to G$ is a bijection with inverse $z\mapsto z-[c]w$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The identities $(r+[c]w)-[c]w=r$ and $(z-[c]w)+[c]w=z$ show that the maps are mutual inverses.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise transcript identity]
If $\varphi(w)=y$, then for every $r\in G$ and $c\in\bool$,
\[
\Rtrans(w,r,c)=\Strans(\tau_{c,w}(r),c).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Put $z=r+[c]w$. Challenge and response coordinates agree. The simulated commitment is
\[
\varphi(z)-[c]y
=\varphi(r)+[c]\varphi(w)-[c]y
=\varphi(r),
\]
which is the real commitment.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge]
Let $G$ be finite and $w$ satisfy $\varphi(w)=y$. For each fixed $c\in\bool$, the multiset
\[
\left\{\!\left\{\Rtrans(w,r,c):r\in G\right\}\!\right\}
\]
equals
\[
\left\{\!\left\{\Strans(z,c):z\in G\right\}\!\right\}.
\]
Consequently, for uniform $r$ and $z$, the real and simulated transcript probability mass functions are identical.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The translation $r\mapsto\tau_{c,w}(r)$ permutes $G$, so reindexing by it preserves the multiset of indices. The pointwise transcript identity pairs every real transcript with an identical simulated transcript, including multiplicity. Dividing multiplicities by $|G|$ gives equality of probability mass functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Zero distinguishing advantage]
For every predicate $D$ on transcripts,
\[
\Pr[D(T_{\mathrm{real}})=1]=\Pr[D(T_{\mathrm{sim}})=1].
\]
Thus every observer, even an unbounded one, has distinguishing advantage zero.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Equal probability mass functions assign equal mass to every event, including the set on which $D$ outputs one.
\end{proof}

The qualifier ``honest-verifier'' is essential. The result fixes a challenge and simulates its prescribed view. A malicious verifier could choose challenges as a function of commitments or auxiliary state; handling this generally needs stronger transformations.

\section{Special soundness}
Knowledge extraction uses two transcripts with one commitment and opposite challenges.

\begin{theorem}[Special soundness]
Suppose $(a,0,z_0)$ and $(a,1,z_1)$ are accepted. Then
\[
w'=z_1-z_0
\]
is a witness: $\varphi(w')=y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Acceptance gives $\varphi(z_0)=a$ and $\varphi(z_1)=a+y$. Hence
\[
\varphi(z_1-z_0)=\varphi(z_1)-\varphi(z_0)=(a+y)-a=y.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-challenge knowledge]
If a commitment $a$ and response rule $A:\bool\to G$ produce an accepted transcript $(a,c,A(c))$ for both challenges, then the statement has a witness, namely $A(1)-A(0)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply special soundness to $A(0)$ and $A(1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Challenge exclusivity]
If no $w\in G$ satisfies $\varphi(w)=y$, no commitment can admit accepted answers to both challenges.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Two accepted answers would yield the forbidden witness by special soundness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Single-round knowledge error]
On a statement without a witness, any prover that fixes its commitment and response behavior before a uniformly random Boolean challenge is accepted with probability at most $1/2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Challenge exclusivity permits acceptance on at most one member of the two-element challenge space.
\end{proof}

This says nothing about the computational difficulty of finding witnesses for true statements; that depends on the concrete homomorphism.

\section{Algorithms}
\paragraph{Honest generation.} Given $\varphi,y,w,r,c$, compute $a=\varphi(r)$ and $z=r+[c]w$. In $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$ with $\varphi(x)=kx\bmod n$, this uses a constant number of modular operations. Modular addition costs $O(\log n)$ bit operations and schoolbook modular multiplication costs $O((\log n)^2)$.

\paragraph{Simulation.} Fix $c$, sample $z$ uniformly, and compute $a=\varphi(z)-[c]y$. This has the same asymptotic arithmetic cost and does not access $w$.

\paragraph{Verification.} Compute $\varphi(z)$ and $a+[c]y$ in canonical representatives and compare. For a multiplication homomorphism modulo $n$, this is one modular multiplication, one modular addition, and one comparison.

\paragraph{Extraction.} Check equal commitments, opposite challenges, and acceptance; then output $z_1-z_0$. Its algebraic core is one subtraction, costing $O(\log n)$ in a cyclic group.

\paragraph{Finite distribution audit.} For a group of size $N$, enumerate all $N$ real tapes and all $N$ simulator responses, tally transcripts, and compare frequency maps. This costs $O(N)$ transcript generations and $O(N)$ storage in the worst case. It illustrates the distribution theorem; the translation bijection avoids enumeration in a mathematical proof.

\section{Numerical examples}
\begin{example}[A true statement modulo $11$]
Let $G=H=\mathbb Z/11\mathbb Z$ and $\varphi(x)=3x\pmod{11}$. Choose $w=4$, giving $y=1$. For $r=7$, the commitment is $a=10$. Challenge zero has response $z_0=7$ and checks $3z_0\equiv10$. Challenge one has response $z_1=7+4\equiv0$ and checks
\[
3z_1\equiv0\equiv10+1=a+y\pmod{11}.
\]
Extraction returns $z_1-z_0\equiv0-7\equiv4$, the witness.

For simulation at challenge one, choose $z=5$ and set $a=3z-y\equiv3$. Verification checks $3z\equiv4\equiv a+y$ modulo $11$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[A false statement modulo $8$]
Let $G=H=\mathbb Z/8\mathbb Z$, $\varphi(x)=2x$, and $y=1$. There is no witness because the image consists of even residues. Challenge-zero acceptance requires $2z_0\equiv a$, while challenge-one acceptance requires $2z_1\equiv a+1$. Since $a$ and $a+1$ have opposite parity, at most one equation is solvable. The simulator can nevertheless create a transcript after fixing either challenge, because it is free to derive the commitment from its chosen response.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and interpretation}
\subsection{Identification and credentials}
The protocol models challenge-response identification. Public data specify $(\varphi,y)$ and the private credential is $w$. The user responds to fresh randomness without directly sending $w$. Practical deployment additionally requires a hard-to-invert homomorphism, secure composition, implementation protections, and a malicious-verifier analysis.

Because an honest verifier can generate an identically distributed transcript alone, its transcript cannot constitute unique evidence that a prover participated. This property is conceptually relevant to privacy-preserving credentials, although production systems use richer relations and adversarial models.

\subsection{Confidential computation}
Homomorphic witness relations are building blocks for proving consistency of hidden values. A participant may establish that secret data obey a public algebraic relation. The present protocol captures one linear relation; practical systems combine constraints and commitments that bind hidden values across many checks.

\subsection{Hidden mathematical proofs}
A mathematical derivation can abstractly be regarded as a witness for an efficiently checkable relation: the theorem statement is public and a valid derivation is private. General zero-knowledge constructions can hide witnesses for suitable relations under appropriate assumptions.

The present protocol, however, is not equivalent to opening a random proof line. One line may leak the central idea, and local validity does not alone imply global correctness. A rigorous construction needs a hiding and binding commitment, a locally checkable encoded proof with quantified soundness, and a zero-knowledge transformation that masks opened views. Communication polynomial only in statement length is also a succinctness demand. It does not follow solely from arithmetizing proofs or from probabilistic local checking, because the hidden proof may be much longer than the statement.

\section{Discussion and limitations}
Three equations organize the protocol:
\[
z=r+[c]w,\qquad a=\varphi(z)-[c]y,\qquad w'=z_1-z_0.
\]
The first generates honest responses, the second simulated commitments, and the third extracted witnesses. Translation masks the witness in one view; subtraction cancels the shared mask across two views.

Perfect zero knowledge and special soundness coexist because the simulator and extractor receive different powers. The simulator fixes one challenge before constructing its commitment. The extractor receives two accepted branches tied to the same prior commitment. One branch can be made from public data; spanning both branches forces a witness.

Finiteness is used only for the exact uniform-distribution statement. Pointwise identity and translation are valid in arbitrary commutative groups. Infinite groups require a chosen probability measure and translation-invariance argument. Commutativity simplifies the additive presentation; noncommutative analogues require careful equation ordering.

The limitations are substantive. Zero knowledge is only for the honest verifier. The protocol is interactive; deriving challenges from hashes requires a separate model. No inversion-hardness claim is made. The single-round false-instance success bound is only $1/2$. Independent repetition should yield $2^{-k}$ after $k$ rounds, but a full theorem must define the repeated strategy and probability space. Finally, arbitrary proof certification demands considerably more than committing to proof lines.

\section{Future work}
The Boolean challenge can be generalized to a finite field. Responses $z=r+cw$ permit extraction from distinct challenges by dividing their difference, under explicit scalar-action and invertibility hypotheses, and should yield knowledge error reciprocal to challenge-space size.

A probability-theoretic development can state equality directly for probability mass functions, define sequential and parallel repetition, and prove reduction of false-instance success from $1/2$ to $2^{-k}$. Concrete finite cyclic groups can then connect the abstract relation to discrete logarithms and explicit hardness assumptions.

Malicious-verifier zero knowledge is a further priority. Its simulator must handle challenges depending on commitments and auxiliary state. Commitment schemes should be specified with explicit hiding and binding games before any commit-and-open proof-certificate protocol claims privacy.

Finally, propositional syntax, proof certificates, and polynomial-time checking can be connected to general zero-knowledge proofs for nondeterministic polynomial time. Extending this vision to arithmetic theories requires precise encodings and complexity parameters. Communication polynomial only in statement length needs succinct-proof machinery beyond arithmetization and local checking.

\section{Conclusion}
The three-move homomorphism protocol is a complete miniature of zero-knowledge reasoning. Honest transcripts always verify. Simulated transcripts also verify. Over finite uniform randomness, translation pairs every genuine transcript with an identical simulated one, proving perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge. Two accepted opposite-challenge responses under one commitment expose a witness by subtraction; absent a witness, a commitment covers at most one challenge and succeeds with probability at most $1/2$.

The mechanism is economical: homomorphic addition proves completeness, translation proves privacy, and subtraction proves knowledge. It provides a rigorous foundation for larger challenge spaces, repetition, concrete hard groups, malicious verifiers, commitments, and privacy-preserving certification of richer claims.

